it's great to be joined today by Glenn  while who's the principal researcher at  Microsoft Research New England and along  with Eric Posner the co-author of the  new book radical markets uprooting  capitalism and democracy for a just  society and maybe we'll start Glenn with  the four adjust society I mean what as  your as you approach the writing of this  book what were the unjust elements of  society that we're kind of like the  framework for for what you wrote well I  think that there are several things that  are going wrong in our society one is  the fact that we have this enormous and  growing inequality so much of the wealth  going to so few people who don't really  make contributions that merit such  disproportional share of the social  wealth another huge problem is the way  that our economy is stagnating and  people have fewer choices less freedom  less opportunities today many people  feel then that then their parents had  and finally maybe most importantly our  society seems to be rife with areas  where minorities are being oppressed by  majorities and we don't have a really  good way to resolve that problem you  know we sometimes have judges try to  protect one group but that ends up  leading to all sorts of political  dissatisfaction and conflict so on all  these levels or society really seems to  be not responding to the demands of  citizens and that's what we're trying to  address here one of the areas maybe we  can we can choose to dive in here is  your second chapter in the book called  radical democracy where you talk about a  system of quadratic voting which I find  super interesting what is a sort of  layman's term a explanation of what  quadratic voting is well the basic  problem as I mentioned that we're trying  to address is that minorities are  usually protected in our current system  by the will of judges but you know  judges aren't really democratically  elected and aren't really accountable to  the public on the other hand majority  rule wouldn't really work because you  think about an issue like gay marriage  that wouldn't be fair to you have  everyone have exactly one vote on that  issue so the question is how do we  resolve this Quadra  voting is a solution where every citizen  would have an equal number of votes but  they wouldn't have to spend all of those  votes all on you know just one vote per  issue or per election if an issue is  more important to someone if someone  knew more about an issue they could  choose to spend more of their vote  credits on that issue and people could  get a lot of votes on the issues most  important less votes on the issues less  important to them and there's this  little twist which is that because you  don't want everyone to spend all their  votes on the one issue the caramels most  that they care most about you actually  get more votes the more evenly you  spread your votes so the cost of votes  in units of these credits is the square  of the number of votes that you get so  in other words if you choose to support  four different initiatives rather than  one you would not end up having four  times the number of votes but I guess  what would it be sixteen or I guess I'm  not exactly sure I'm visualizing the  math yeah it depends it depends exactly  on here's here's an example um if you  have a hundred credits and there are ten  issues we actually ran a survey like  this if you put all your credits on one  issue you'd get 10 votes on that issue  because 10 is the square root of 100 but  if you put equal numbers of votes on all  the issues you'd get roughly 3 or 4  votes on every issue so that's like 30  votes so you see by spreading things out  more equal you get almost three times as  much total influence but in a more  diffuse way across issues so you know  I'm preparing for the interview I read  critiques of some of your ideas and the  one that seemed to come up often with  the quadratic voting is the idea that  this fundamentally violates one-person  one-vote and that idea but I know that  that that's an issue that that you  address so how do you address that issue  well so the question is what do we value  about one person one vote  is it the rigidity that every person has  to have exactly one vote on every issue  or is it the fact that we all should be  equals politically I actually think that  if it's the latter which is what I think  we should care about then quadratic  voting is actually more true to that  principle because you think about  minorities if if you're  they say an african-american living in  the inner city in the United States you  don't have equality just because you  have the same one-person one-vote on  every issue you might get gerrymandered  and even beyond that you just you know  you're a minority and so you're gonna  lose on a whole variety of issues where  majorities can rule it's much more  consistent with the principle of  equality that each of us gets an equal  total amount of voice but that we can  choose to concentrate that voice on  issues that are most important to us so  that we have greater freedom to push for  the things that really matter and really  affect our interests I'm curious whether  quadratic voting and some of the other  ideas in the book which which we can we  can get into as well in your in your  mind is sort of the underlying  philosophy a sort of utilitarianism  where you're sort of measuring outcomes  and whether we call it marginal utility  to society or the economy I don't really  know but in reading it my thought was  that there's a very utilitarian  underpinning although it's sort of like  a social utilitarianism of some kind or  am I just way off yeah well I think a  lot of people have that perception and  certainly utilitarianism and that sort  of thinking was one way that we helped  that helped us come up with these ideas  but I wouldn't say that I'm a  utilitarian or that the book relies on  you being a utilitarian because we  approach things from many different  perspectives I mean some people are  utilitarian some people care about  inequality for its own sake some people  care about I'm not having oppressive  authorities that dominate people but not  so much about utility exactly our book  helps advance all of those goals and we  try to argue for it from all those  different perspectives there's something  in religion that's called syncretism  which is that many different people  might all worship the same God but they  view different aspects of it and this  book is written very much in that spirit  we don't want to take too strong of a  stand on whether it's utilitarianism or  autonomy or Liberty or things like this  because we believe that these ideas can  help improve along all  those dimensions and we aren't gonna  resolve you know philosophical or  religious disputes in a book like this  what we're instead gonna do is propose  new ways of organizing our society to  advance values that many of us share  some of the ideas seem at first glance  more difficult pragmatically to even  think about how they might be  implemented in your first chapter you  talk about common common ownership self  assess tax which we could talk about in  more detail potentially you also talk  about ways of uniting the world workers  with a visa system where individuals  would be able to sponsor visas for  people from other countries which of  these I'm curious do you think would be  the most and I know this is not your  focus but which would be the most  pragmatically viable if you had to  choose one where we could most  approximate change the soonest is there  one oh yeah I think that what I would  say is it depends a lot on whether you  mean implement the full extent of the  proposal or where can we make the most  near-term progress in I'd say in terms  of better society  yeah so the data is labor chapter in  which we argue that people should be  able to receive compensation for the  contributions that they make to the  machine learning and artificial  intelligence systems of companies like  Google and Facebook rather than just  having that data be taken from them is  probably the most palatable and widely  appreciated of the ideas and probably  the one that will get off the ground you  know at a broad scale the quickest so  the idea would be all of the data all of  your activity that's taking place on  platforms which couldn't be the Google  universe the Facebook universe what  could be any of these that you would  somehow as a contributor of your data to  that ecosystem be compensated along what  lines are we talking about literally  financial compensation yeah eventually  absolutely and you know we imagine  building what we call a data labor  movement once people wake up get quote  cyber woke to the value that their data  is creating and this  digital economy and the way that the AI  systems that are supposed to replace our  jobs are actually based on all of our  contributions people will start  demanding payment through sort of labor  unions of a new modern digital sort and  fair terms of both use and compensation  can be negotiated that way yeah that's  interesting because there's often the  idea that listen Facebook Ads don't work  on me in the sense that I haven't ever  bought anything which looked let's  assume that that's true although it can  sometimes be hard to figure out exactly  why you did buy something at some point  in your life so I'm of no value so I  just don't really care about this but  the truth is that the algorithm with  which successful targets of a product or  surface but we're targeted was data that  did come from you and thus it does have  some economic value even if you're not  actually buying stuff yeah and it's not  just the targeting of ads I mean that's  what we're all familiar with today but  going forward the greatest value these  companies are going to create is not  from advertising really instead it's  from the artificial intelligent systems  that they're training on our data and  that they'll sell the companies to  automate  for example I don't know if you saw  Google's recent new assistant yet the  process of you know making reservations  for someone so they're gonna automate  that and their training all that based  on our data so people are going to be  losing their jobs and no one's going to  be getting a new job even though all  that that system relies on input that we  put in the book is fascinating it's  called radical markets uprooting  capitalism and democracy for a just  society Glenn thanks so much for talking  to me about this today thanks a lot  David

